watchOS 27.2 could introduce a feature that lets Apple Watch screenshots be previewed with options to share or delete, similar to how screenshots work on the iPhone.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/watchos-27-2-screenshot.jpg" alt="" width="1500" height="843" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1067240" /><br/>
Signs of the feature were found in watchOS 27.2 by <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2103169846364000344">code explorer pdfu</a>. The option is hidden behind a feature flag that's not enabled, but it could be introduced in a later watchOS 27.2 beta.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><blockquote class="twitter-tweet"><p lang="en" dir="ltr">A new screenshot experience is coming to Apple Watch, inspired by iPhone.<br><br>Hidden behind a feature flag in watchOS 27.2, it lets you preview, share, or delete a screenshot right after taking it. <a href="https://t.co/MT5wmDjRnq">pic.twitter.com/MT5wmDjRnq</a></p>&mdash; pdfu (@itspdfu) <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2103169846364000344?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">September 24, 2026</a></blockquote> <script async src="https://platform.x.com/widgets.js" charset="utf-8"></script></div><br/>
Right now, taking a screenshot on the Apple Watch sends it directly to the iPhone <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/photos/">Photos</a> app with no confirmation screen. It's easy to accidentally take a screenshot since it only requires pressing the Digital Crown and side button at the same time.<br/>
<br/>
On the iPhone, taking a screenshot shows either a full-screen preview or a thumbnail preview depending on your settings, but both options let you save, share, or delete the screenshot with a couple taps.<br/>
<br/>
